  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will continue through the rest of the \nweek with a day to day warming trend.\n\nBroad upper level ridging continues across the central and eastern \nCONUS through the remainder of the week that will support overall \ndry weather across western NY. With generally southwesterly flow, \n850mb temps continue to rise near 8-9 degC Thursday and over 10 degC \nSaturday, above the climatological 90th percentile for mid-November. \nAfternoon highs look to reach the upper 50s to low 60s for much of \nthe area Thursday. Friday will be slightly cooler and breezy with a \nlow pressure system passing to our north across Canada, but will \nwarm back up for Saturday. Surface winds turning more southerly with \nsurface high pressure building across the Northeast US will limit \nany lake cooling influence and providing a downsloping component to \nsupport temperatures reaching the mid-60s in spots, around 15 degF \nabove normal for this time of the year.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next chance for precipitation along with cooler, but \nseasonable, air arrives late this weekend into early next week. \n\nBy late this weekend, the upper level pattern begins to change as \nthe ridging shifts off to the east with an approaching cold front. \nThis front will bring the chance for showers to the area Sunday \nnight into Monday along with a colder airmass that could support a \nlake response early next week with rain and/or snow showers south of \nthe lakes. This cooler airmass will help return western NY to \nseasonable temperatures for late November, but plenty of uncertainty \nremains with the timing of the front, interactions with a coastal \nstorm's development, and the depth of the cold air mass. Guidance \nhas been trending slight slower with the cold front's passage and \n850mb temperature spread at this time based on ensemble \nguidance remains quite high behind the front.",
